        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Le, un... or du?",
              "body": "French has three kinds of articles. \"Le / la / les\" point to something specific or general (the). \"Un / une / des\" count things you can count (a, some). For things you don't count — like bread, milk or jam — French uses the partitive: \"du\" (masculine), \"de la\" (feminine), \"de l'\" before a vowel.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "Je mange un croissant.", "english": "I eat a croissant.", "note": "countable → un" },
                { "french": "Je mange du pain.", "english": "I eat some bread.", "note": "du + masculine" },
                { "french": "Je mange de la confiture.", "english": "I eat some jam.", "note": "de la + feminine" },
                { "french": "Je bois de l'eau.", "english": "I drink some water.", "note": "de l' + vowel" },
                { "french": "Elle mange des céréales.", "english": "She eats some cereal.", "note": "des + plural" },
                { "french": "Il mange un œuf.", "english": "He eats an egg.", "note": "countable → un" }
              ],
              "tip": "English can drop \"some\" (I eat bread), but French cannot drop the article: Je mange du pain."
            }
          ]
        },
